<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!-- A floating CONTROL capsule on the flight screen (design decision).

     WHAT IT REPLACED: a solid #0D47A1 bar, 56dp tall, across the WHOLE width, with a darker
     2dp edge (bg_toolbar). It was opaque chrome over the one thing the pilot flies from, and
     once the HUD readouts lost their panels it was the only element left on the screen still
     separating itself with a solid background.

     The controls now sit in two capsules that float on the video — status on the left, actions
     on the right — and the strip between them is picture again.

     WHY A FILL HERE AND AN OUTLINE ON THE READOUTS: a capsule covers a small patch, so it can
     afford to be opaque, and the icons inside it keep their own colours (green for connected,
     red for live, amber for caution) which an outline would fight. The readouts cover far more
     of the picture and carry no colour of their own, so they take the outline instead. Same
     philosophy, different answer per element — see tp_hud_outline.

     ⚠ THE RADIUS IS THE LARGE ONE ON PURPOSE. hud_control_radius says "you touch this"; a
     square or barely-rounded edge says "you read this" — the mini-map frame, the readouts.
     Keeping the two apart is what makes the screen legible at a glance. -->
<shape xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:shape="rectangle">

    <solid android:color="@color/tp_hud_capsule" />

    <corners android:radius="@dimen/hud_control_radius" />
</shape>
